  <h2>Waterfront and canal lots</h2>
  <p>Lots on Lake St. Clair, Anchor Bay and the canals have the strictest rules:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>St. Clair Shores:</strong> privacy fences are not permitted on any waterfront or canal lot (Code Sec. 8-253(11)).</li>
    <li><strong>Chesterfield Township:</strong> no privacy fences outside the building envelope on any waterfront or canal lot. In the water-side front yard of lots on Anchor Bay and the Salt River (south of Callens Road), only non-obscuring decorative aluminum or wrought iron up to 48 inches is allowed. No walls, hedges, chain link or obscuring fences.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In practice, that makes <a href="/services/aluminum-fencing/">black aluminum</a> the go-to waterfront fence.</p>

  <h2>Corner lots and sight lines</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>St. Clair Shores:</strong> up to 6′6″ along the side street from the rear lot line to the front of the house, then down to 4′6″, sloping to no more than 2′6″ at the front lot line, in a material that doesn’t block traffic sight lines.</li>
    <li><strong>Harper Woods:</strong> up to 6′ on the street-side lot line, extending to the front building line. Nothing over 30″ above the curb inside the corner visibility triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Chesterfield Township:</strong> street-side yard fences set back at least 5′ from the right-of-way, and out of a 15′ clear-vision triangle at roads and driveways.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Which side faces out?</h2>
  <p>Most ordinances here require the finished side to face neighbors and the street: Harper Woods, Macomb Township and Clay Township among them. St. Clair Shores also requires any "bad side" to face the installer’s property. A <a href="/services/wood-fencing/">shadow box</a> or <a href="/services/vinyl-fencing/">vinyl</a> fence looks the same from both sides, which avoids the issue.</p>

  <h2>Other rules worth knowing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Post depth (St. Clair Shores):</strong> at least 42″ below grade, in concrete or by another accepted method.</li>
    <li><strong>Bottom gap (St. Clair Shores):</strong> no more than 3″ between grade and the fence.</li>
    <li><strong>No double fencing (Macomb Township):</strong> a new fence must be at least 3½′ from any other fence. No spiked or pointed tops.</li>
    <li><strong>Lot-line consent (Harper Woods):</strong> a fence on the lot line needs written consent from all adjacent owners, and two fences can’t share posts.</li>
    <li><strong>Materials (Clay Township):</strong> treated wood, plastic, aluminum, galvanized metal or similar. No chicken wire or snow fence as permanent fencing.</li>
    <li><strong>Barbed wire and electric fences</strong> are prohibited on residential fences in St. Clair Shores, Chesterfield Township, Harper Woods and Clay Township (outside farm use).</li>
    <li><strong>Pools:</strong> pool barriers are governed by the building code, not the fence ordinance.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Call MISS DIG 811 before anyone digs</h2>
  <p>Michigan law requires a MISS DIG 811 locate request at least <strong>3 business days</strong> before any digging, whatever the size or depth. When a contractor does the digging, the contractor places the ticket. Utilities don’t mark private lines, such as wiring to a shed, sprinkler lines or an invisible dog fence. More in our <a href="/resources/preparing-for-fence-installation/">installation prep checklist</a>.</p>

